00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:22.510
So one slide-- what
is Cloud Spanner?

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:24.700
So we say it's the
best of two worlds

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:28.750
of the relational semantics
and having horizontal scale.

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:30.430
So there's a lot
of technology going

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:33.970
on underneath Cloud Spanner
which makes that possible.

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:38.350
Traditionally, if you look at
horizontally scaling databases,

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:40.180
there are two basically flavors.

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:44.680
Either you go and sacrifice some
of the relational semantics,

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:47.200
and with this the
consistency constrains,

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:50.680
or you might end up with a
lot of conflict resolution,

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:57.070
where maybe a system that you
have to particular co-locate

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:59.320
to get a good
performance out of it

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:02.470
because of the communication
that is necessary to ensure

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:04.040
the strong consistency.

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:05.890
Now, under Cloud Spanner,
there is a system

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:08.410
called TrueTime, which
allows us to remove

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:12.130
some of the communication
necessary to ensure

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:14.590
strong consistency.

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:18.680
So let's dive into the
planning aspect of it.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:21.160
So if you look at
traditional database,

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:23.060
traditional
relational databases,

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:25.570
and that is usually
a single-host system.

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:27.530
So they are vertical, scalable.

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:30.580
If you need more power,
you put more CPUs in,

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:33.130
you put more main memory
in, but at some point,

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:36.170
there is a ceiling what
you can scale vertically.

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:38.470
Now, in these
single-host systems,

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:41.530
over the last 40,
50 years by now,

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:44.710
they have a lot of cool
features developed,

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:46.810
but which are
specifically tailored

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:49.270
for a single-host
system, and which

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:53.230
are detrimental to a
system which you distribute

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:56.140
horizontally over multiple
machines, even zones

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:57.710
and regions.

00:04:57.710 --> 00:04:59.600
So some of them I mention here.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:01.510
So if you look at
traditional databases,

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:03.980
you have things like value
constraints, you can check,

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:06.220
you can do foreign
key constraints.

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:08.600
And with this
referential integrity,

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:12.520
you have sequences, which you
can use for your primary key,

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:15.190
for instance, and it's
nothing bad to use them.

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:17.380
It's actually sometimes
really helpful

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:20.770
to use these sequences
in single-host systems.

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:23.920
You have possibilities
for user-defined functions

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:27.880
to do verification, to do type
conversion, things like that.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:31.390
You have stored procedures where
you can put business logic in.

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:34.090
And you have triggers
where you can basically

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:37.870
trigger certain
processes asynchronously

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:42.200
over multiple tables, or even
synchronously if you want to.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:44.620
And then also a
feature which is very

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:46.640
common in traditional
databases is

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:51.130
that you have very fine,
granular permission systems.

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:54.310
So most databases support
permission systems

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:57.910
on databases, on
tables, and then there

00:05:57.910 --> 00:06:02.740
are a few that go down to row
or even cell level permission

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:05.150
systems in your database.

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:07.960
Now, how does this
compare to Cloud Spanner?

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:10.030
Number one, you
have no sequences.

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:12.940
So the way that Cloud
Spanner is storing data

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:16.810
is we basically order the
table alphanumerically

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:20.320
by the primary key,
and then we partition

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:22.240
or we split this
table up, and we

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:25.360
move these splits
to different hosts

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:28.030
to be responsible
for these splits.

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:30.410
Actually, it's not one
host that is responsible.

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:32.270
It's a Paxos group
that is responsible.

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:34.960
So it's three machines,
usually three machines,

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:37.870
that are responsible for one
split, which is basically

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:39.460
a part of your table.

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:42.130
Now, since we are ordering
them alphanumerically

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:45.040
by the primary key, if
you would use a sequence

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:47.050
as a primary key,
you can imagine

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:52.690
that new data would always
be appended at the end,

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:54.610
so it always goes
to the same split.

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:59.170
And by having only one split be
responsible for all the load,

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:03.100
you run into hotspotting,
because only one in one Paxos

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:05.180
group is responsible
for that one split,

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:07.510
and so the boundaries
of your scalabilities

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:11.620
are basically what you
can put in terms of load

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:13.600
onto this split and
these machines that

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:15.920
are working on that.

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:19.260
We have also now things like
foreign key constraints,

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:21.430
because they can
be really expensive

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:23.720
if you need to do
these verification

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:27.880
of referential integrity
across multiple splits,

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:30.980
maybe even across
multiple regions.

00:07:30.980 --> 00:07:33.220
So we have something
which is kind

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:35.620
of a foreign key constraint
minus, minus, which

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:37.510
we call interleaved tables.

00:07:37.510 --> 00:07:41.470
So you can actually interleave
a table into another table.

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:43.730
Because they are living
on the same split,

00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:46.660
we can actually do this
referential integrity there,

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:51.530
and we can do things like delete
on cascade and stuff like that.

00:07:51.530 --> 00:07:53.350
Another thing that
we don't currently

00:07:53.350 --> 00:07:55.180
support in Cloud
Spanner are things

00:07:55.180 --> 00:07:58.240
like triggers,
stored procedures,

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:00.160
and user-defined functions.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:02.410
All these things have
to basically move up

00:08:02.410 --> 00:08:06.130
into your application layer
to have the same functionality

00:08:06.130 --> 00:08:07.960
for your application
if you want to move

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:10.930
from a traditional database
system to Cloud Spanner.

00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:13.470
And then the next
thing is the user

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:15.880
permission systems that we
support on Cloud Spanner

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:18.770
is currently on
the database level.

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:21.100
So you can create
a service account

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:23.950
and can give a database
write and read levels,

00:08:23.950 --> 00:08:26.210
but you can't go
any lower than that.

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:28.570
So all the user
control that you have,

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:32.510
all the access control that
you want to support, again,

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:34.390
needs to move into
your application layer.

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:39.270
There are a couple of
other things to mention.

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:41.010
So if you look, for
instance, at the data

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:42.509
types that Cloud
Spanner supports,

00:08:42.509 --> 00:08:46.230
there is a good
variety of data types.

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:49.200
But we hear, loud and clear,
and we are looking into that

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:52.500
and working on it to
support a numeric type.

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:55.410
Especially a numeric type
for financial applications,

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:57.270
also scientific
applications, where

00:08:57.270 --> 00:09:01.140
you need a lot of
accuracy and precision,

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.090
a numeric type is
basically something

00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:05.580
what you want to have in your
database natively supported.

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:07.140
Currently, we don't support it.

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:10.590
So our advice, if you
move from a system

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:12.750
which imports a numeric
type to Cloud Spanner

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:15.060
to store these things in string.

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:18.870
Float and integer will not
give you enough precision

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:23.850
unless you are basically
distributing your numerics

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:27.870
across multiple integers
or multiple floats.

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:30.990
Again, this also is in line
with the node sequences.

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:34.500
If you look at the primary keys,
to enable the distribution,

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:37.890
to enable the
horizontal scale, you

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:40.660
want to have very
well-distributed primary keys.

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:45.360
So if you come from MySQL or
PostgreSQL or any of the larger

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:48.150
third-party databases
that are out there,

00:09:48.150 --> 00:09:50.400
and you're using
something like a sequence

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:54.360
or you're using
continuous generated key

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:58.800
for your primary keys, you
need to basically modify that.

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:04.680
So use something like UUID
version 4, or prefix your key

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:09.010
with something which
is well-distributed.

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:12.660
Another important thing to
look at in Cloud Spanner

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:17.460
is that advanced structures,
like indexes, for instance,

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:21.840
are actually mapped
back to basic structures

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:22.620
in Cloud Spanner.

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:25.260
So indexes are
just normal tables

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:28.570
in our back end [INAUDIBLE],,
which you don't see as such.

00:10:28.570 --> 00:10:31.575
You see them as indexes,
but we are actually

00:10:31.575 --> 00:10:33.330
handling them as tables.

00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:34.600
And this is important here.

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:36.240
If you look at
traditional databases

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:38.540
and you look at
indexes, how they work,

00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:43.020
you basically build up, usually
like a HashMap or something,

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:46.240
where you can go
do an index scan.

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:50.250
And if you find your key or
what you're looking for, and you

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:52.620
want to map the row
against the-- like,

00:10:52.620 --> 00:10:56.710
get some other data out of
your base table for that index

00:10:56.710 --> 00:10:57.410
lookup--

00:10:57.410 --> 00:11:00.150
you basically just do a
jump either in memory,

00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:02.400
or you know where it
is located on disk,

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:04.860
and you map that
into your memory,

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:06.900
and then basically
can directly access

00:11:06.900 --> 00:11:10.560
the data of your base table.

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:13.410
Now, since data is
distributed in Cloud Spanner,

00:11:13.410 --> 00:11:18.255
we don't have these advantages
on that level for lookups.

00:11:18.255 --> 00:11:22.560
You can't just go to a
different memory location

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:25.830
on a different host to do
the lookup of the base table

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:28.060
data for a certain row.

00:11:28.060 --> 00:11:31.530
So what actually happens
is you do the index lookup.

00:11:31.530 --> 00:11:33.870
It's just a table, basically.

00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:37.380
And then if you don't have
the data in your index

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:39.730
that you actually want to
access with your query,

00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:42.510
you do have to do a back-join
into the base table.

00:11:42.510 --> 00:11:45.880
And a back-join in a distributed
system can be very expensive.

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:49.950
So be aware of this, that
if you create indexes,

00:11:49.950 --> 00:11:53.190
and you want to actually have
some that are providing you

00:11:53.190 --> 00:11:55.440
the data that qualifies
for the query,

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:59.070
there are capabilities to
store that data with the index,

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:01.350
so it's basically stored
in the same table,

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:06.170
and so you do not need
to do the back-joins.

00:12:06.170 --> 00:12:09.790
So what are the
workloads, four of them,

00:12:09.790 --> 00:12:12.710
which are fitting well
onto Cloud Spanner?

00:12:12.710 --> 00:12:15.200
So one of them is,
if you have already

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:17.970
a sharded relational
database system,

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:23.720
you're probably already in the
weeds of managing this, looking

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:27.400
at how you shard, maybe you
do have to repartition things,

00:12:27.400 --> 00:12:28.560
stuff like that.

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:31.520
So it's usually a lot of work
to do these sharded systems.

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:35.000
But because you have a sharded
system, many of the things

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:38.900
that I mentioned earlier, like
UDFs or stored procedures,

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:40.610
you might already
not using anymore,

00:12:40.610 --> 00:12:44.010
because they don't work well
in these sharded systems.

00:12:44.010 --> 00:12:46.820
So you might have gotten
rid of these things

00:12:46.820 --> 00:12:50.720
already in your database layer
and have moved them already

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:52.290
to the application layer.

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:55.220
So if that is the case, the
transition and migration,

00:12:55.220 --> 00:12:59.040
the move to Cloud Spanner, might
be much, much easier for you.

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:01.850
Another thing is if you are
at the edge of scalability,

00:13:01.850 --> 00:13:04.670
I mentioned, if you're at the
edge of vertical scalability,

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:07.670
and you want to
basically consider

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:10.220
to move into a NoSQL
world because you need

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:13.880
the horizontal scale, that is
also a good use case where you

00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:15.050
can look into Cloud Spanner.

00:13:15.050 --> 00:13:16.880
You keep the
relational semantics,

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:21.010
but you get the horizontal
scale on top of it.

00:13:21.010 --> 00:13:24.750
The third one is manageability
and high availability.

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:27.980
So if you have a system
where you run, for instance,

00:13:27.980 --> 00:13:30.650
your master and then
multiple failover systems

00:13:30.650 --> 00:13:33.890
because you need to be high
available in case of there is

00:13:33.890 --> 00:13:37.490
any data corruption or
machine fault or anything,

00:13:37.490 --> 00:13:39.380
and you failover to
a different machine,

00:13:39.380 --> 00:13:43.790
you usually get
some offline time

00:13:43.790 --> 00:13:46.460
during that process to
failover and to catch up

00:13:46.460 --> 00:13:48.330
on locks and things like that.

00:13:48.330 --> 00:13:52.130
So this is a case, also, which
is a very good case for using

00:13:52.130 --> 00:13:54.800
Cloud Spanner, because it's
distributed by default.

00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:58.670
It's high available by default.
You have the regional instances

00:13:58.670 --> 00:14:01.790
with four nines of availability
and the multi-region instances

00:14:01.790 --> 00:14:05.210
with five nines of availability.

00:14:05.210 --> 00:14:08.240
And with the five
nines of availability

00:14:08.240 --> 00:14:11.180
comes the multi-region
capability of Cloud Spanner.

00:14:11.180 --> 00:14:14.120
So we have configurations
where your data

00:14:14.120 --> 00:14:18.440
lives in multiple regions that
we support at Google Cloud

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:19.550
regions.

00:14:19.550 --> 00:14:23.240
And the responsibility
for data elements

00:14:23.240 --> 00:14:27.480
is basically moved
between these two regions.

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:31.310
And then if one region
fails, all the responsibility

00:14:31.310 --> 00:14:33.680
for that data moves
to the other region.

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:35.780
And your data-- your
database lives on

00:14:35.780 --> 00:14:41.490
is still accessible
from that other region.

00:14:41.490 --> 00:14:44.250
So what are things that don't
fit well on Cloud Spanner?

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:48.420
So if you are heavily invested
with stored procedures, UDFs,

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:51.640
things like that into your
traditional database system,

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:54.270
and you want to basically pick
it up and drop it onto Cloud

00:14:54.270 --> 00:14:56.610
Spanner, you won't be happy.

00:14:56.610 --> 00:14:58.510
There will be a
lot of challenges.

00:14:58.510 --> 00:15:02.530
It might actually not even
work for various reasons.

00:15:02.530 --> 00:15:05.460
So lift and shift is not really
a thing for Cloud Spanner.

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:10.830
It is a lift and re-platform,
re-applicate, basically.

00:15:10.830 --> 00:15:13.530
Another thing is if you're
using your application,

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:16.320
like ORM and things like
that, we are looking

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:18.850
and we are investing
into ORM support.

00:15:18.850 --> 00:15:22.440
But at the moment, we don't
have a lot of ORM support.

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:24.600
So if you look
into compatibility

00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:27.780
of what you have on
traditional database systems,

00:15:27.780 --> 00:15:31.660
in terms of library, that is
just not in that state yet

00:15:31.660 --> 00:15:32.970
for Cloud Spanner.

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:35.100
And then if you look
into applications

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:37.470
where you need really,
really low latencies,

00:15:37.470 --> 00:15:41.340
like single-digit
milliseconds or even down

00:15:41.340 --> 00:15:43.590
to the nano and
microseconds, that's

00:15:43.590 --> 00:15:46.140
also not an application
for Cloud Spanner because

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:51.360
of the nature that we
focus on, high availability

00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:53.580
and manageability.

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:56.760
And so the way that Cloud
Spanner is built underneath

00:15:56.760 --> 00:15:59.310
to have the durability
of your data just

00:15:59.310 --> 00:16:04.200
doesn't allow for the very
low single-digit millisecond

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:05.760
latencies.

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:07.380
So another thing
that you don't want

00:16:07.380 --> 00:16:09.330
to do with Cloud Spanner,
which I added there

00:16:09.330 --> 00:16:11.340
on the fourth point,
is Cloud Spanner

00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:14.020
is not an all-app database.

00:16:14.020 --> 00:16:17.370
So if you do heavyweight
analytical queries,

00:16:17.370 --> 00:16:19.622
you will run into problems.

00:16:19.622 --> 00:16:22.570
It will be painful, and
you won't be happy with it.

00:16:22.570 --> 00:16:24.360
So for that, I
encourage everybody

00:16:24.360 --> 00:16:27.430
to look into BigQuery
or even Bigtable.

00:16:27.430 --> 00:16:29.430
If you have something
that you need a little bit

00:16:29.430 --> 00:16:33.330
more real-time analytics, look
into these two storage systems

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:36.320
that we have available
as well to help you

00:16:36.320 --> 00:16:39.210
with your analytical needs.

00:16:39.210 --> 00:16:43.410
So just kind of in summary,
what are the things

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:45.760
that you want to look
at if you do your schema

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:48.600
migration from your
old application

00:16:48.600 --> 00:16:51.570
on a traditional database
system to Cloud Spanner?

00:16:51.570 --> 00:16:54.510
So if you have monotonic
keys, prefix them,

00:16:54.510 --> 00:16:59.880
use well-distributed keys, and
use interleaving carefully.

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:02.010
So as mentioned,
interleaved tables

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:06.490
are ending up in the same
split as the parent row.

00:17:06.490 --> 00:17:10.410
So if you have a lot of load
on an interleaved table for one

00:17:10.410 --> 00:17:14.440
row of your parent table, you
basically go to one split,

00:17:14.440 --> 00:17:19.589
and so you are in danger of
hotspotting to that split.

00:17:19.589 --> 00:17:22.730
In terms of indexes,
again, what we

00:17:22.730 --> 00:17:26.780
see a lot is that
customers are basically

00:17:26.780 --> 00:17:27.829
looking at the schema.

00:17:27.829 --> 00:17:29.545
They're fixing
their primary keys.

00:17:29.545 --> 00:17:30.170
They are happy.

00:17:30.170 --> 00:17:31.650
Everything is great.

00:17:31.650 --> 00:17:34.310
And then you create an
index on the updated row

00:17:34.310 --> 00:17:37.190
or on the insert row
or insert time row.

00:17:37.190 --> 00:17:40.380
So as mentioned, an
index is also a table.

00:17:40.380 --> 00:17:42.620
And if you create an index
on a monotonic field,

00:17:42.620 --> 00:17:45.890
you run into the
exact same problems.

00:17:45.890 --> 00:17:48.500
So be aware of this,
that indexes behave

00:17:48.500 --> 00:17:52.010
the same as your tables, and
you need to pay attention

00:17:52.010 --> 00:17:55.520
to what you use as your
primary key for your tables

00:17:55.520 --> 00:17:57.740
as well as for your indexes.

00:17:57.740 --> 00:18:01.050
And then if you
have things like--

00:18:01.050 --> 00:18:02.690
an example is a social network.

00:18:02.690 --> 00:18:04.410
You already have
a really hot key.

00:18:04.410 --> 00:18:07.340
There is one popular person,
which everybody wants

00:18:07.340 --> 00:18:09.140
to know something about it.

00:18:09.140 --> 00:18:13.520
Consider replicating and
duplicating that key,

00:18:13.520 --> 00:18:16.550
and build the logic around
that into your application

00:18:16.550 --> 00:18:20.300
to make sure that it's still
strong, consistent, updated

00:18:20.300 --> 00:18:21.410
across all your replicas.

00:18:24.370 --> 00:18:35.530
So another thing-- what is
very common, what we see

00:18:35.530 --> 00:18:40.210
is that there are 10 tables
created during runtime,

00:18:40.210 --> 00:18:45.040
or anything kind of like
schema changes very often done.

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:48.970
New applications are
deployed very frequently.

00:18:48.970 --> 00:18:51.500
And with every
application deployment,

00:18:51.500 --> 00:18:53.600
there is a schema change.

00:18:53.600 --> 00:18:55.850
So you have to be careful
with schema changes.

00:18:55.850 --> 00:18:58.227
And Cloud Spanner supports
online schema changes.

00:18:58.227 --> 00:19:00.310
So you don't have any
downtime that you experience

00:19:00.310 --> 00:19:02.230
if you do a schema change.

00:19:02.230 --> 00:19:06.373
But we keep schemas
around for around an hour.

00:19:06.373 --> 00:19:08.290
And the reason is you
can actually travel back

00:19:08.290 --> 00:19:09.975
in time with Cloud Spanner.

00:19:09.975 --> 00:19:12.940
These are called
time-bounded reads, which are

00:19:12.940 --> 00:19:14.780
used for backups, for instance.

00:19:14.780 --> 00:19:21.100
But also if you want to use a
little bit less locks and less

00:19:21.100 --> 00:19:23.470
contention, you can do
time-bounded reads or

00:19:23.470 --> 00:19:25.420
time-bounded queries.

00:19:25.420 --> 00:19:29.980
So be aware of that, that if
you do a lot of schema changes,

00:19:29.980 --> 00:19:31.403
that they basically stack up.

00:19:31.403 --> 00:19:33.070
And we keep them all
in memory, and that

00:19:33.070 --> 00:19:36.230
puts a lot of memory
pressure on our system.

00:19:36.230 --> 00:19:39.760
So use them wisely
and use them sparsely.

00:19:39.760 --> 00:19:43.450
Try to aggregate
your schema changes.

00:19:43.450 --> 00:19:46.110
As mentioned there, schema
changes are serialized.

00:19:46.110 --> 00:19:48.880
So they're put on a
stack and then rolled out

00:19:48.880 --> 00:19:49.600
after each other.

00:19:49.600 --> 00:19:51.100
And they are transactional.

00:19:51.100 --> 00:19:53.170
So we basically do
all the schema changes

00:19:53.170 --> 00:19:54.100
in the background.

00:19:54.100 --> 00:19:56.620
And then once the
new schema is ready,

00:19:56.620 --> 00:19:58.630
we flip it over
with a transaction.

00:19:58.630 --> 00:20:01.480
And any transactions
that are in flight,

00:20:01.480 --> 00:20:04.480
which would span two
versions of the schema,

00:20:04.480 --> 00:20:08.230
we basically just abort it
and rerun that transactions.

00:20:08.230 --> 00:20:10.360
And then for schema
changes, if you

00:20:10.360 --> 00:20:12.400
have new indexes
that you create,

00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:18.220
these indexes can take days
even if you backfill that index

00:20:18.220 --> 00:20:19.420
from very large tables.

00:20:19.420 --> 00:20:22.360
So be aware of that as well.

00:20:22.360 --> 00:20:25.330
So with all the things, all
the constraints, and all

00:20:25.330 --> 00:20:27.710
the challenges that
Cloud Spanner has,

00:20:27.710 --> 00:20:29.800
if you move onto
Cloud Spanner, there

00:20:29.800 --> 00:20:31.283
are some good things about it.

00:20:31.283 --> 00:20:32.950
There are actually a
lot of good things.

00:20:32.950 --> 00:20:36.310
I am working on Cloud Spanner
for two and a half years now.

00:20:36.310 --> 00:20:39.480
It is definitely one of
my favorite products.

00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:42.670
I have a lot of fun
playing with it.

00:20:42.670 --> 00:20:46.530
Benefit given, I don't have to
pay out of my pocket for it.

00:20:46.530 --> 00:20:48.940
But it is a lot of
fun to play with it.

00:20:48.940 --> 00:20:51.790
So what do you get, actually,
if you move onto Cloud Spanner?

00:20:51.790 --> 00:20:53.710
You get a fully managed system.

00:20:53.710 --> 00:20:55.870
So that is important
not to forget.

00:20:55.870 --> 00:20:58.810
So you don't need
to do any upgrades.

00:20:58.810 --> 00:21:00.340
You don't have downtimes.

00:21:00.340 --> 00:21:06.040
You don't have downtimes
within the SLA.

00:21:06.040 --> 00:21:09.530
You don't have any software
patching that you have to do.

00:21:09.530 --> 00:21:11.050
You don't have to
do any resharding.

00:21:11.050 --> 00:21:12.850
All this is done for you.

00:21:12.850 --> 00:21:14.170
We do the load balancing.

00:21:14.170 --> 00:21:17.200
We do the optimizations
of the splits and things

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:21.480
like that behind the scenes
based on your traffic patterns.

00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:25.020
And really important is
you get strong consistency

00:21:25.020 --> 00:21:28.770
across a horizontally
distributed system.

00:21:28.770 --> 00:21:32.290
So true asset capabilities
and strong consistency,

00:21:32.290 --> 00:21:36.420
which is the highest
guarantee of consistency

00:21:36.420 --> 00:21:37.420
that you have out there.

00:21:37.420 --> 00:21:41.460
So we don't believe in choosing
your consistency level.

00:21:41.460 --> 00:21:44.400
We just give you the highest.

00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:45.660
So what to consider.

00:21:45.660 --> 00:21:48.180
This is basically
the planning phase.

00:21:48.180 --> 00:21:50.370
So these are the things
that you have to consider.

00:21:50.370 --> 00:21:53.490
Is the application that you're
running right now, is it

00:21:53.490 --> 00:21:54.510
suitable to move?

00:21:54.510 --> 00:21:57.360
How much do I have to
invest in terms of work?

00:21:57.360 --> 00:21:59.280
What work do I have
to invest in terms

00:21:59.280 --> 00:22:02.680
of schema changes, application
changes, things like that?

00:22:02.680 --> 00:22:04.820
Now, if you decide
and say, yes, there

00:22:04.820 --> 00:22:06.820
are all these benefits
that come with it,

00:22:06.820 --> 00:22:08.700
and I want to move
under Cloud Spanner,

00:22:08.700 --> 00:22:12.450
here are a couple of ideas
how to approach this.

00:22:12.450 --> 00:22:16.030
So number one, and that
might sound very simplistic,

00:22:16.030 --> 00:22:17.910
but I still want to
put it out there,

00:22:17.910 --> 00:22:20.670
the easiest way to move your
application to Cloud Spanner

00:22:20.670 --> 00:22:25.850
is to bundle all your database
access code into one module.

00:22:25.850 --> 00:22:29.460
So with that, you can do
a lot of amazing things.

00:22:29.460 --> 00:22:33.060
You can basically isolate
the testing for that module.

00:22:33.060 --> 00:22:36.540
You can optimize around that
module in terms of latency,

00:22:36.540 --> 00:22:41.070
in terms of contention,
in terms of ACLs

00:22:41.070 --> 00:22:42.660
that you can implement
in this model.

00:22:42.660 --> 00:22:44.970
So it makes it
much easier, if you

00:22:44.970 --> 00:22:47.100
have the possibility
in your application,

00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:50.550
to isolate the DB access code
and move that all into one

00:22:50.550 --> 00:22:54.750
module, so that it makes the
transition from your old system

00:22:54.750 --> 00:22:59.470
to the new system much
more straightforward.

00:22:59.470 --> 00:23:01.320
So now, how would
you do that in terms

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:03.540
of getting your data over?

00:23:03.540 --> 00:23:07.200
One possibility is
to use Cloud Dataflow

00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:12.060
or use a backup that you can
use on MySQL or PostgreSQL

00:23:12.060 --> 00:23:15.210
or on your favorite database
system that you're using.

00:23:15.210 --> 00:23:19.170
So basically, we do
a backup into files.

00:23:19.170 --> 00:23:20.730
They can have different formats.

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:26.320
If it is CSV, if it is Avro, if
it's Parquet, it's up to you.

00:23:26.320 --> 00:23:29.070
And then you move that
basically into Cloud Storage,

00:23:29.070 --> 00:23:31.950
into a Cloud Storage
packet, and then from there

00:23:31.950 --> 00:23:36.000
you use the recommended ways
to use Cloud Dataflow to load

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:37.870
this data into Cloud Spanner.

00:23:37.870 --> 00:23:39.810
And I'm going to
show a little bit.

00:23:39.810 --> 00:23:45.390
Please switch in a
second to my laptop.

00:23:45.390 --> 00:23:49.268
If I remember of my password Oh.

00:23:53.440 --> 00:23:53.940
Whew.

00:23:53.940 --> 00:23:55.210
OK.

00:23:55.210 --> 00:23:58.910
Let's do caffeine, which
I would need as well.

00:23:58.910 --> 00:24:01.000
So what I want to
show here is, if you

00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:03.940
want to get started with
Dataflow to load data

00:24:03.940 --> 00:24:05.950
into your Cloud
Spanner instance,

00:24:05.950 --> 00:24:09.790
we have actually some Dataflow
templates available for you

00:24:09.790 --> 00:24:11.320
that you can look
into and basically

00:24:11.320 --> 00:24:15.680
take as an inspiration or even
modify it in a way as you want.

00:24:15.680 --> 00:24:19.340
So on GitHub, on the Google
Cloud Platform organization,

00:24:19.340 --> 00:24:21.550
you'll find Dataflow templates.

00:24:21.550 --> 00:24:23.860
And as you can see
here, we have a bunch

00:24:23.860 --> 00:24:28.720
of templates which allow
you to basically export

00:24:28.720 --> 00:24:33.220
into, for instance,
Avro, CSV, or text files,

00:24:33.220 --> 00:24:38.290
and then also load from GCS
text into Cloud Spanner.

00:24:41.740 --> 00:24:44.350
So this is a good
place to start.

00:24:44.350 --> 00:24:45.850
And these are
actually the templates

00:24:45.850 --> 00:24:48.940
that we are also using for
our self-managed backup

00:24:48.940 --> 00:24:50.407
that you have available.

00:24:50.407 --> 00:24:51.990
So I want to show
that really quickly.

00:24:51.990 --> 00:24:54.310
So what is possible
is actually here

00:24:54.310 --> 00:24:59.590
I have a bunch of my instances
that are running right now.

00:24:59.590 --> 00:25:01.280
I have done already a restore.

00:25:01.280 --> 00:25:03.010
So I'm going to show
that in a second.

00:25:03.010 --> 00:25:07.120
But basically, what you can do
is you can go onto a database,

00:25:07.120 --> 00:25:12.370
click the Export button,
select the bucket where

00:25:12.370 --> 00:25:13.390
you want to export it.

00:25:13.390 --> 00:25:15.580
So I have here my
