00:26:29.410 --> 00:26:32.470
Now, if you go into
the Import, again, I

00:26:32.470 --> 00:26:35.500
can show the dialog in a second.

00:26:35.500 --> 00:26:36.200
It's very easy.

00:26:36.200 --> 00:26:39.820
You pick basically the folder
where you exported it to,

00:26:39.820 --> 00:26:44.200
and then it creates the
database schema automatically

00:26:44.200 --> 00:26:46.390
and then loads into this data.

00:26:46.390 --> 00:26:49.948
Now, some might spot there
is a green line over here

00:26:49.948 --> 00:26:51.490
and the blue line
over here, and they

00:26:51.490 --> 00:26:54.980
don't match up, which is
usually not a good sign.

00:26:54.980 --> 00:26:56.870
And that is also the
reason why we see here

00:26:56.870 --> 00:26:59.890
it took an hour to
restore that data.

00:26:59.890 --> 00:27:02.410
Again, this can
be improved a lot

00:27:02.410 --> 00:27:06.920
by having sufficient quota
for your Dataflow jobs.

00:27:06.920 --> 00:27:09.830
So as you can see here, I
didn't have a sufficient quota.

00:27:09.830 --> 00:27:15.190
So at around 25 workers, I
was maxed out on my quota.

00:27:15.190 --> 00:27:18.220
And the green line is
actually the suggested scaling

00:27:18.220 --> 00:27:21.550
of the Dataflow job in terms
of nodes that are helping

00:27:21.550 --> 00:27:23.720
or workers that are
helping loading this data.

00:27:23.720 --> 00:27:27.190
So if I would have
had more quota,

00:27:27.190 --> 00:27:30.760
this restore job would
have gone much faster.

00:27:30.760 --> 00:27:35.350
But again, it's
not something that

00:27:35.350 --> 00:27:37.870
is automated in the
background for us

00:27:37.870 --> 00:27:39.320
in terms of how do you schedule.

00:27:39.320 --> 00:27:42.250
You have to schedule your
own backups and restores

00:27:42.250 --> 00:27:44.410
if you want to use this.

00:27:44.410 --> 00:27:46.690
So let me go back really quick.

00:27:46.690 --> 00:27:50.690
So how would that look like
if you have, for instance,

00:27:50.690 --> 00:27:52.090
an instance running here?

00:27:52.090 --> 00:27:57.360
You would go to your
bucket, ticketshop-backups.

00:27:57.360 --> 00:28:00.395
Select the folder.

00:28:04.240 --> 00:28:07.480
For instance, let's say, demo01.

00:28:07.480 --> 00:28:11.690
Select the region, confirm,
import, and then it

00:28:11.690 --> 00:28:15.890
basically, again,
creates this Dataflow job

00:28:15.890 --> 00:28:17.000
you see here already.

00:28:17.000 --> 00:28:18.380
The schema is created.

00:28:18.380 --> 00:28:19.970
And in a second,
it should come up

00:28:19.970 --> 00:28:21.860
that we have this Dataflow job.

00:28:21.860 --> 00:28:24.950
You can go here, look at
it, and it creates that all

00:28:24.950 --> 00:28:29.030
behind the scenes, and I don't
need to do anything more.

00:28:29.030 --> 00:28:31.980
Let's switch back to
the slides, please.

00:28:34.590 --> 00:28:39.230
So if you don't want to use
the Dataflow job to load

00:28:39.230 --> 00:28:40.700
your data into
Cloud Spanner, you

00:28:40.700 --> 00:28:43.010
want to run your own
import into Cloud Spanner,

00:28:43.010 --> 00:28:44.600
there are a couple
of best practices

00:28:44.600 --> 00:28:46.010
that you want to look at.

00:28:46.010 --> 00:28:51.140
Number one, what we see a lot
is you create a new instance,

00:28:51.140 --> 00:28:54.350
you create a new
database, and then you

00:28:54.350 --> 00:28:57.450
throw all the load basically
against that database.

00:28:57.450 --> 00:28:59.800
What you will see is
that in the beginning,

00:28:59.800 --> 00:29:04.640
it will take a while to actually
catch up and scale nicely.

00:29:04.640 --> 00:29:07.260
And the reason for this is
you create your new instance

00:29:07.260 --> 00:29:08.750
and new database
with your schema,

00:29:08.750 --> 00:29:11.300
all your tables are
empty, and all your tables

00:29:11.300 --> 00:29:13.390
have exactly one split.

00:29:13.390 --> 00:29:15.110
So up to the point
that we actually

00:29:15.110 --> 00:29:18.110
have a nice splitting of the
tables, which can accommodate

00:29:18.110 --> 00:29:22.430
the load, you will see
basically not your best

00:29:22.430 --> 00:29:24.330
possible performance.

00:29:24.330 --> 00:29:28.080
So if you actually want to
move over your live data,

00:29:28.080 --> 00:29:30.470
your production data in
a really quick manner

00:29:30.470 --> 00:29:34.350
from your old system to the
new system, what we recommend

00:29:34.350 --> 00:29:37.940
is that you create your
database, the schema,

00:29:37.940 --> 00:29:41.120
and then load it with
generated data or with some--

00:29:41.120 --> 00:29:46.080
maybe you can use a backup
to preload that database.

00:29:46.080 --> 00:29:49.880
Then delete all the data,
and then load the actual data

00:29:49.880 --> 00:29:51.930
that you want to
load into there.

00:29:51.930 --> 00:29:54.920
The reason is, with
preloading your database,

00:29:54.920 --> 00:29:57.140
you're basically
pre-warming all the splits,

00:29:57.140 --> 00:30:01.400
and that enables it to go
much faster than you actually

00:30:01.400 --> 00:30:04.130
need the higher performance.

00:30:04.130 --> 00:30:08.210
Second thing is group and
batch multiple rows together

00:30:08.210 --> 00:30:10.490
in a transaction.

00:30:10.490 --> 00:30:13.190
The optimal size, between
1 and 10 megabytes.

00:30:13.190 --> 00:30:16.130
And we have a limitation
of 20,000 mutations.

00:30:16.130 --> 00:30:17.600
This is a hard limitation.

00:30:17.600 --> 00:30:22.980
We won't remove that for
the foreseeable future.

00:30:22.980 --> 00:30:26.030
So you have to stay in this
20,000 mutations range.

00:30:26.030 --> 00:30:27.365
What is a mutation?

00:30:27.365 --> 00:30:31.920
A mutation is any cell change
that you have in your tables.

00:30:31.920 --> 00:30:37.460
So imagine you have a table
with five columns and three

00:30:37.460 --> 00:30:38.620
primary keys.

00:30:38.620 --> 00:30:42.050
And then you have
another index, which

00:30:42.050 --> 00:30:46.000
is using the primary keys,
just in a different order.

00:30:46.000 --> 00:30:49.130
And let's say, also, for
all the three primary keys.

00:30:49.130 --> 00:30:53.330
Actually, for any row that you
insert into that base table,

00:30:53.330 --> 00:30:54.800
you have eight mutations.

00:30:54.800 --> 00:30:57.320
Five are coming
from your base table

00:30:57.320 --> 00:30:59.870
and three are coming
from the index table.

00:30:59.870 --> 00:31:02.210
So have that in mind, like,
if you have a lot of indexes

00:31:02.210 --> 00:31:05.270
in there and you want to load
a lot of data into your table,

00:31:05.270 --> 00:31:09.290
you might run quickly into the
20,000 mutations limitation.

00:31:09.290 --> 00:31:13.400
Then if you do the batching
and loading into Cloud Spanner,

00:31:13.400 --> 00:31:16.070
this might sound
counterintuitive, but actually,

00:31:16.070 --> 00:31:19.070
order your data that you want
to load into Cloud Spanner

00:31:19.070 --> 00:31:23.180
by primary key, and then split
it up into distinct ranges,

00:31:23.180 --> 00:31:26.330
and then send these distinct
ranges to Cloud Spanner.

00:31:26.330 --> 00:31:28.700
That gives you the
best throughput

00:31:28.700 --> 00:31:31.650
to load into Cloud Spanner
and then, of course,

00:31:31.650 --> 00:31:35.280
scale out your import processes.

00:31:35.280 --> 00:31:38.310
So two types of
migrations that we can do.

00:31:38.310 --> 00:31:42.390
Number one, of course, is
doing an application migration

00:31:42.390 --> 00:31:44.040
with downtime.

00:31:44.040 --> 00:31:46.580
So here, basically, you
have your application

00:31:46.580 --> 00:31:48.590
talking to your original DB.

00:31:48.590 --> 00:31:51.440
You do all the export, and
import it into Cloud Spanner.

00:31:51.440 --> 00:31:54.070
You have your new
application ready.

00:31:54.070 --> 00:31:59.810
And so you turn your application
down, then you do the backup,

00:31:59.810 --> 00:32:01.970
and restore into Cloud Spanner.

00:32:01.970 --> 00:32:04.220
And then you turn the
new application on

00:32:04.220 --> 00:32:08.680
and let the new application
speak to Cloud Spanner.

00:32:08.680 --> 00:32:10.750
Simple, but with downtime.

00:32:10.750 --> 00:32:13.420
Now, it's a little
bit more complicated

00:32:13.420 --> 00:32:15.550
if you actually want
to have a system that

00:32:15.550 --> 00:32:18.130
runs without
downtime and you want

00:32:18.130 --> 00:32:24.940
to have an online migration,
then you do all the schema

00:32:24.940 --> 00:32:26.230
adoption as well.

00:32:26.230 --> 00:32:28.420
You deploy your new
application basically,

00:32:28.420 --> 00:32:31.180
which has both versions,
can speak to the old

00:32:31.180 --> 00:32:33.610
and to the new database.

00:32:33.610 --> 00:32:37.390
You do an export, which
is basically the preload,

00:32:37.390 --> 00:32:40.360
while your application
still uses the original DB

00:32:40.360 --> 00:32:42.790
as the original source
of truth, and then

00:32:42.790 --> 00:32:45.510
you implement basically
a Dataflow job,

00:32:45.510 --> 00:32:48.370
for instance, which does
the change data capturing

00:32:48.370 --> 00:32:51.070
from your original database
into Cloud Spanner.

00:32:51.070 --> 00:32:54.610
And as soon as they basically
catch up and they are in sync,

00:32:54.610 --> 00:32:58.930
you can flip the switch and
let your application talk

00:32:58.930 --> 00:33:02.290
to the Cloud Spanner,
and hit the Launch button

00:33:02.290 --> 00:33:05.590
and have a launch party,
and cross the fingers

00:33:05.590 --> 00:33:08.170
that everything works fine.

00:33:08.170 --> 00:33:12.740
So in summary, isolate DB
code, do the schema updates

00:33:12.740 --> 00:33:16.240
upfront with the best
practices that I mentioned,

00:33:16.240 --> 00:33:20.170
move your business logic
to the application,

00:33:20.170 --> 00:33:23.110
run your old and new
databases in parallel

00:33:23.110 --> 00:33:25.330
if you want to do the
online, and then basically do

00:33:25.330 --> 00:33:26.020
the switch over.

00:33:26.020 --> 00:33:28.780
And test that multiple
times before you do it

00:33:28.780 --> 00:33:32.300
actually on your customers.

00:33:32.300 --> 00:33:34.420
So that concludes
section one, planning.

00:33:34.420 --> 00:33:36.520
Section two, how you
would efficiently

00:33:36.520 --> 00:33:38.500
move your data to
the Cloud Spanner.

00:33:38.500 --> 00:33:42.920
And the third one is monitoring,
inspecting, and optimizing.

00:33:42.920 --> 00:33:46.780
So I have a couple of
demos that I show here.

00:33:46.780 --> 00:33:50.710
Of course, again, I could
only pick very specific ones.

00:33:50.710 --> 00:33:54.010
I would love to do an
entire session only

00:33:54.010 --> 00:33:58.960
about the index modifications
and optimizations,

00:33:58.960 --> 00:34:02.120
but I probably would
lose 80% of the room,

00:34:02.120 --> 00:34:04.250
and that I want to avoid.

00:34:04.250 --> 00:34:07.540
So I have concentrated
on something simple.

00:34:07.540 --> 00:34:10.210
So I want to set
the stage first.

00:34:10.210 --> 00:34:11.960
So this is the stage
that I want to set.

00:34:11.960 --> 00:34:13.400
So this is a schema.

00:34:13.400 --> 00:34:14.980
It's fairly straightforward.

00:34:14.980 --> 00:34:22.960
I designed this schema for
the 2017 launch keynote demo

00:34:22.960 --> 00:34:24.130
for Cloud Spanner.

00:34:24.130 --> 00:34:26.949
So you can see, we
reuse things sometimes.

00:34:26.949 --> 00:34:29.050
I love to reuse this schema.

00:34:29.050 --> 00:34:32.659
It allows me to do a couple
of really nice demos.

00:34:32.659 --> 00:34:34.179
So what we have here is--

00:34:34.179 --> 00:34:35.889
what I created as
a demo application

00:34:35.889 --> 00:34:38.920
is like a simulated ticket shop.

00:34:38.920 --> 00:34:44.230
And a ticket shop basically
uses a global distributed,

00:34:44.230 --> 00:34:47.239
multi-regional, global
Spanner instance.

00:34:47.239 --> 00:34:50.770
And you can buy tickets in
a strong, consistent manner

00:34:50.770 --> 00:34:52.699
from all over the world.

00:34:52.699 --> 00:34:54.300
The cool thing here
is I am not going

00:34:54.300 --> 00:34:57.730
to sell a ticket twice, which
happens for some airlines,

00:34:57.730 --> 00:35:02.323
I heard, and other places, which
is not necessarily the best

00:35:02.323 --> 00:35:04.990
thing to have for your customer,
when they are standing in front

00:35:04.990 --> 00:35:08.170
of the airplane door
and you are not allowed

00:35:08.170 --> 00:35:09.850
in because the airplane's full.

00:35:09.850 --> 00:35:12.070
So a strong,
consistent system here

00:35:12.070 --> 00:35:16.930
can help greatly if you actually
can sell the tickets exactly

00:35:16.930 --> 00:35:17.780
that you want.

00:35:17.780 --> 00:35:20.680
Now, some might walk up
to me afterwards and say,

00:35:20.680 --> 00:35:21.980
they do that on purpose.

00:35:21.980 --> 00:35:24.020
I know they do that on
purpose, because there

00:35:24.020 --> 00:35:27.085
are a lot of people
that don't show up.

00:35:27.085 --> 00:35:28.747
Now, what do I want to show?

00:35:28.747 --> 00:35:30.580
I have three tables
that we're going to look

00:35:30.580 --> 00:35:33.280
at a little bit more in detail.

00:35:33.280 --> 00:35:34.870
I have my Venue table.

00:35:34.870 --> 00:35:36.820
So I have 1,000 venues
all over the world

00:35:36.820 --> 00:35:39.280
that I generated artificially.

00:35:39.280 --> 00:35:41.260
I have a Seating category.

00:35:41.260 --> 00:35:44.740
It looks much better on
this display than on this.

00:35:44.740 --> 00:35:46.420
I have the
SeatingCategory there,

00:35:46.420 --> 00:35:49.600
which basically tells me, like,
is this a general seating?

00:35:49.600 --> 00:35:51.040
Is this a VIP seating?

00:35:51.040 --> 00:35:52.810
Is it a lounge?

00:35:52.810 --> 00:35:54.340
Is it a saloon?

00:35:54.340 --> 00:35:55.470
Do you get dinner with it?

00:35:55.470 --> 00:35:56.610
Something like that.

00:35:56.610 --> 00:35:58.760
And then I have
the actual tickets.

00:35:58.760 --> 00:36:01.860
So the tickets basically
are the tickets.

00:36:01.860 --> 00:36:03.880
In that case, I have
pre-created these tickets,

00:36:03.880 --> 00:36:06.490
so that I just need
to flip some bits if I

00:36:06.490 --> 00:36:08.120
want to sell a ticket.

00:36:08.120 --> 00:36:10.870
So the commonalities in these
tables are, in my Venue table,

00:36:10.870 --> 00:36:13.420
I have 1,000 venues.

00:36:13.420 --> 00:36:17.590
In my seating categories, I
have 2,655 seating categories,

00:36:17.590 --> 00:36:22.340
so roughly 2.6 seating
categories per venue.

00:36:22.340 --> 00:36:23.330
And that's an average.

00:36:23.330 --> 00:36:25.870
There are some venues which
only have one seating category.

00:36:25.870 --> 00:36:27.820
There are others
which have five.

00:36:27.820 --> 00:36:29.830
And then I have my tickets.

00:36:29.830 --> 00:36:32.980
If I can count correctly,
this is about 20,000 tickets

00:36:32.980 --> 00:36:35.970
that I have in my database.

00:36:35.970 --> 00:36:41.960
Now, what I want to show
is I want to basically do

00:36:41.960 --> 00:36:44.220
optimization of an index.

00:36:44.220 --> 00:36:47.240
So what I'm going
to show first is

00:36:47.240 --> 00:36:49.520
I'm going to show
a query that I have

00:36:49.520 --> 00:36:55.740
run, which I started actually
before I started this talk.

00:36:55.740 --> 00:37:00.060
And so you can see,
to explain the query,

00:37:00.060 --> 00:37:04.190
I want to know how many tickets
are available in the Berlin Old

00:37:04.190 --> 00:37:06.620
Club, which is my venue name.

00:37:06.620 --> 00:37:12.050
So to find out, I basically
have to go to my venue,

00:37:12.050 --> 00:37:17.090
join it with my SeatingCategory,
join it with the Ticket table,

00:37:17.090 --> 00:37:18.820
and then do the select.

00:37:18.820 --> 00:37:22.580
And the ones that are
familiar over here

00:37:22.580 --> 00:37:24.950
in this room with SQL,
they cringe probably

00:37:24.950 --> 00:37:27.890
right now the way that
I've written this query.

00:37:27.890 --> 00:37:30.930
And I've written that
on purpose this way.

00:37:30.930 --> 00:37:33.080
So basically, what
you see here is

00:37:33.080 --> 00:37:36.830
I'm doing a join from my
SeatingCategory onto the Ticket

00:37:36.830 --> 00:37:39.870
table and then onto the Venue.

00:37:39.870 --> 00:37:42.080
So now, I could make
that even worse.

00:37:42.080 --> 00:37:43.910
If I would switch
and say, I want

00:37:43.910 --> 00:37:47.600
to join from my Ticket
table to my SeatingCategory

00:37:47.600 --> 00:37:51.230
and then to the Venue, my
query wouldn't actually finish.

00:37:51.230 --> 00:37:52.070
It times out.

00:37:52.070 --> 00:37:52.670
I tested.

00:37:52.670 --> 00:37:54.750
Trust me.

00:37:54.750 --> 00:37:56.330
It times out.

00:37:56.330 --> 00:38:00.410
And so I did the second
worst option here.

00:38:00.410 --> 00:38:02.420
I basically take
my SeatingCategory,

00:38:02.420 --> 00:38:04.490
join my Ticket,
and join the Venue.

00:38:04.490 --> 00:38:06.470
The problem that
we have here is I

00:38:06.470 --> 00:38:08.930
have a pretty big
cardinality table

00:38:08.930 --> 00:38:12.590
that I joined basically in
the beginning and the smaller

00:38:12.590 --> 00:38:15.800
cardinality table
towards the end.

00:38:15.800 --> 00:38:19.250
As you can see here,
this took four minutes

00:38:19.250 --> 00:38:22.543
to basically finish.

00:38:22.543 --> 00:38:23.960
And I have a couple
of things that

00:38:23.960 --> 00:38:25.280
are actually pretty nice here.

00:38:25.280 --> 00:38:29.360
So you see the rows scanned,
how much CPU time I'm using.

00:38:29.360 --> 00:38:33.110
It shows you basically the query
plan here that you can see,

00:38:33.110 --> 00:38:35.240
OK, I'm doing a table
scan on the Venue,

00:38:35.240 --> 00:38:37.560
then I'm using an index on
the SeatingCategory, which

00:38:37.560 --> 00:38:38.850
is already good.

00:38:38.850 --> 00:38:41.360
I'm using an index on my
Ticket table, which is good,

00:38:41.360 --> 00:38:44.180
but I'm still going
through all my tickets.

00:38:44.180 --> 00:38:47.730
So in the end, it's fairly slow.

00:38:47.730 --> 00:38:52.280
So I want to show the power
of actually improving just

00:38:52.280 --> 00:38:54.440
by reordering the
way that you are

00:38:54.440 --> 00:38:57.750
joining your tables together.

00:38:57.750 --> 00:39:00.230
So in this case, I'm
doing the better thing.

00:39:00.230 --> 00:39:04.730
So always, as a
rule of thumb, begin

00:39:04.730 --> 00:39:08.630
with the smallest cardinality
table if you do joins.

00:39:08.630 --> 00:39:11.720
So here I'm starting
with my Venue table,

00:39:11.720 --> 00:39:13.300
and then join the
SeatingCategory,

00:39:13.300 --> 00:39:17.130
and then join the
Ticket table on it.

00:39:17.130 --> 00:39:20.090
So now if we run it,
we're going to see

00:39:20.090 --> 00:39:23.510
it will take still
a little bit while,

00:39:23.510 --> 00:39:30.050
if the demo gods are with me,
and it will show in a second.

00:39:30.050 --> 00:39:32.630
I think it should be
around 25 seconds.

00:39:32.630 --> 00:39:37.190
But basically, you see here,
that's still not great.

00:39:37.190 --> 00:39:38.910
It's only 1,000 venues.

00:39:38.910 --> 00:39:40.670
This should go much faster.

00:39:40.670 --> 00:39:42.600
What is happening here?

00:39:42.600 --> 00:39:44.120
Oh, 15 seconds.

00:39:44.120 --> 00:39:45.350
That is awesome.

00:39:45.350 --> 00:39:49.220
So we went down from 240
seconds to 15 seconds

00:39:49.220 --> 00:39:51.260
just by reordering the joins.

00:39:51.260 --> 00:39:52.910
So think about that.

00:39:52.910 --> 00:39:57.440
If you run into problems, into
issues, into latency and query

00:39:57.440 --> 00:40:00.610
times issues, look at,
what are the cardinalities

00:40:00.610 --> 00:40:01.970
of your tables?

00:40:01.970 --> 00:40:05.700
We know a great optimizer
does that automatically.

00:40:05.700 --> 00:40:08.310
We don't have the
greatest optimizer yet.

00:40:08.310 --> 00:40:09.980
We are working on it.

00:40:09.980 --> 00:40:13.970
But there's still a little bit
of manual labor involved here

00:40:13.970 --> 00:40:16.190
if you want to get
good query times.

00:40:16.190 --> 00:40:19.940
Now, as you can
see, we are still

00:40:19.940 --> 00:40:24.500
scanning 20 million rows here,
which is still way too much.

00:40:24.500 --> 00:40:28.590
It takes us 15 seconds
to actually do this.

00:40:28.590 --> 00:40:31.550
So what I did is--

00:40:31.550 --> 00:40:33.870
I hope this is the right one.

00:40:33.870 --> 00:40:36.710
What I did, actually, I
looked at the Ticket table

00:40:36.710 --> 00:40:38.180
and saw, like,
OK, can I actually

00:40:38.180 --> 00:40:41.190
create an index which
helps me in this?

00:40:41.190 --> 00:40:45.290
So I created an index
which is on the CategoryId,

00:40:45.290 --> 00:40:50.610
because I want to join
with my SeatingCategory.

00:40:50.610 --> 00:40:53.620
And then I have the TicketId,
so I can join the Ticket table

00:40:53.620 --> 00:40:55.410
onto it.

00:40:55.410 --> 00:40:58.420
And then you see something
else in the bottom.

00:40:58.420 --> 00:41:01.340
So I have some more data
actually in the bottom.

00:41:01.340 --> 00:41:03.460
And so what I can
do in this index is,

00:41:03.460 --> 00:41:06.880
I can actually declare
what I want to index on,

00:41:06.880 --> 00:41:08.840
and then I can
attach data to it.

00:41:08.840 --> 00:41:12.230
So this is the STORING clause
that I mentioned earlier.

00:41:12.230 --> 00:41:14.890
So if we show the
DDL, you can see here,

00:41:14.890 --> 00:41:19.240
you basically have
this STORING clause,

00:41:19.240 --> 00:41:21.370
and that allows you
to basically attach

00:41:21.370 --> 00:41:26.508
some data to your primary
key in this index.

00:41:26.508 --> 00:41:27.550
And that is very helpful.

00:41:27.550 --> 00:41:31.240
Now, some might ask, why do
I do it in storing and not

00:41:31.240 --> 00:41:33.400
into the primary key?

00:41:33.400 --> 00:41:34.970
There can be multiple reasons.

00:41:34.970 --> 00:41:36.370
Number one is you
change actually

00:41:36.370 --> 00:41:38.440
the ordering of your
index if you put them

00:41:38.440 --> 00:41:42.250
into the primary key
of the index columns.

00:41:42.250 --> 00:41:43.630
So that is something
to consider.

00:41:43.630 --> 00:41:48.190
There are some other things
that are behind the scenes which

00:41:48.190 --> 00:41:50.840
are considered, basically,
if they are indexed columns

00:41:50.840 --> 00:41:52.550
versus stored columns.

00:41:52.550 --> 00:41:57.320
So now let's have a look at
the query, how it runs here.

00:41:57.320 --> 00:42:01.630
And as we can see, it runs
now in 100 milliseconds,

00:42:01.630 --> 00:42:03.520
gives me the same data back.

00:42:03.520 --> 00:42:08.020
There is 1,650 seats
in my Berlin Old Club.

00:42:08.020 --> 00:42:10.870
And if we look at how many
rows were actually scanned,

00:42:10.870 --> 00:42:14.290
there were only 1,652 scans.

00:42:14.290 --> 00:42:17.730
So we scanned two rows too much.

00:42:17.730 --> 00:42:20.740
We still have potential
for improvement here.

00:42:20.740 --> 00:42:24.940
But as you can see, we went down
from 20 million rows and 240

00:42:24.940 --> 00:42:29.020
seconds, and I didn't even
do the worst kind of query,

00:42:29.020 --> 00:42:32.860
down to milliseconds by
creating the right indices

00:42:32.860 --> 00:42:35.230
and using the right join
order for your queries.

00:42:40.150 --> 00:42:44.170
So let's switch
back to the slides.

00:42:44.170 --> 00:42:51.430
So I want to repeat
again on the indexes,

00:42:51.430 --> 00:42:53.900
because it's something
that is often forgotten.

00:42:53.900 --> 00:42:56.810
If you do have indexes and
use the STORING clause,

00:42:56.810 --> 00:42:59.300
you basically avoid
with the Storing clause

00:42:59.300 --> 00:43:03.815
to do a back-join
against the base table.

00:43:03.815 --> 00:43:08.210
So query is where you do select
star of something really bad.

00:43:08.210 --> 00:43:10.190
Really bad.

00:43:10.190 --> 00:43:11.590
So don't do a select star.

00:43:11.590 --> 00:43:15.200
Be really focused on
these, in your select,

00:43:15.200 --> 00:43:17.280
what columns you really need.

00:43:17.280 --> 00:43:20.210
And then if you expect
or hope an index

00:43:20.210 --> 00:43:22.100
to be used for
your queries, make

00:43:22.100 --> 00:43:24.530
sure that the index
has all the columns

00:43:24.530 --> 00:43:26.600
that you need for your query.

00:43:26.600 --> 00:43:33.600
So as soon as there's
one column not available,

00:43:33.600 --> 00:43:38.090
you create the back-join
to the base table.

00:43:38.090 --> 00:43:39.560
Another thing that
we see often is

00:43:39.560 --> 00:43:42.200
in an index where
you define an index,

00:43:42.200 --> 00:43:47.240
and then you use a subprimary
key or subindex columns,

00:43:47.240 --> 00:43:48.900
but you don't use the prefix.

00:43:48.900 --> 00:43:52.930
So let's say, you do
have a primary key, ABC,

00:43:52.930 --> 00:43:57.440
and you index on
BCA, and then you

00:43:57.440 --> 00:44:04.080
do a query on C. It basically
can't use your index.

00:44:04.080 --> 00:44:08.620
You have to basically go back to
your table and do a table scan.

00:44:08.620 --> 00:44:11.060
So make sure that
in your queries,

00:44:11.060 --> 00:44:18.160
you use the same prefix of
indexed columns for both.

00:44:18.160 --> 00:44:22.900
And in terms of locks, in
terms of complex transactions,

00:44:22.900 --> 00:44:27.980
make sure that the locks are
on the range and column level.

00:44:27.980 --> 00:44:32.220
So make sure that you restrict
that to the actually only used

00:44:32.220 --> 00:44:33.650
and needed data.

00:44:33.650 --> 00:44:38.590
So if you do a large range,
you lock the entire table.

00:44:38.590 --> 00:44:40.510
So make sure that
the ranges are small

00:44:40.510 --> 00:44:42.430
in your complex transactions.

00:44:42.430 --> 00:44:45.520
The way to do that is
actually to do a query first

00:44:45.520 --> 00:44:50.950
to get IDs or get primary
key ranges for the things

00:44:50.950 --> 00:44:54.520
where you want to actually
write data to, and then

00:44:54.520 --> 00:44:56.830
do that in your
read-write transaction

00:44:56.830 --> 00:44:58.960
to really restrict that.

00:44:58.960 --> 00:45:02.170
And also, to get the
benefit of caching,

00:45:02.170 --> 00:45:07.170
make sure that you use something
like bounded parameters.

00:45:07.170 --> 00:45:09.390
Another thing that is
helpful to understand

00:45:09.390 --> 00:45:12.750
is to see how transactions
and locking is

00:45:12.750 --> 00:45:15.180
working in Cloud Spanner.

00:45:15.180 --> 00:45:18.270
So I want to dive into
two types of transactions

00:45:18.270 --> 00:45:20.170
that Cloud Spanner
is supporting.

00:45:23.550 --> 00:45:26.440
Number one is the
single split write.

00:45:26.440 --> 00:45:29.640
For a single split write, the
advantage here is what we have,

00:45:29.640 --> 00:45:32.190
if you do actually
a blind write,

00:45:32.190 --> 00:45:34.110
we can actually
sequence your writes,

00:45:34.110 --> 00:45:37.620
and we can share the
write lock on that split.

00:45:37.620 --> 00:45:38.890
And that is really powerful.

00:45:38.890 --> 00:45:42.870
We basically just use TrueTime,
which is our global clock

00:45:42.870 --> 00:45:44.520
time that we use
in Cloud Spanner,

00:45:44.520 --> 00:45:46.860
to sequence your
transaction, and we still

00:45:46.860 --> 00:45:50.970
have strong consistency and
don't lock on your writes.

00:45:50.970 --> 00:45:52.110
So how does it work here?

00:45:52.110 --> 00:45:55.710
You see here, we have
the Spanner front end.

00:45:55.710 --> 00:45:59.190
It's the API front end
that your client talks to.

00:45:59.190 --> 00:46:02.940
That does a zone lookup
and a split lookup

00:46:02.940 --> 00:46:05.040
on where the split lives.

00:46:05.040 --> 00:46:07.020
This is usually
very heavily cached.

00:46:07.020 --> 00:46:09.660
So the second time that
it comes in the front end,

00:46:09.660 --> 00:46:12.100
it will respond to that already.

00:46:12.100 --> 00:46:14.160
So this happens very
rarely, actually,

00:46:14.160 --> 00:46:16.110
the first two lines
that you see there.

00:46:16.110 --> 00:46:18.270
Then we send the
commit requests.

00:46:18.270 --> 00:46:19.740
We acquire a write log.

00:46:19.740 --> 00:46:22.320
We assign a TrueTime timestamp.

00:46:22.320 --> 00:46:25.710
We are waiting out the
time till that upper bound

00:46:25.710 --> 00:46:27.360
has passed basically.

00:46:27.360 --> 00:46:29.880
And during that time, we
send a persist request

00:46:29.880 --> 00:46:33.750
to the replicas, which are also
responsible for that split.

00:46:33.750 --> 00:46:36.660
As soon as the majority comes
back, we basically send--

00:46:36.660 --> 00:46:40.710
we wait for the TrueTime to
pass, and then respond back

00:46:40.710 --> 00:46:43.360
that it was successful.

00:46:43.360 --> 00:46:45.180
Now, in the multi-split
write, what we

00:46:45.180 --> 00:46:49.440
have here is a scenario where
I do a read on the key 100,

00:46:49.440 --> 00:46:52.080
and then I do a write
to key 100, 300,

00:46:52.080 --> 00:46:57.790
and 400 for the values
ABC respectively.

00:46:57.790 --> 00:47:01.680
And so what I do here, again,
we do the Spit Leader lookup.

00:47:01.680 --> 00:47:03.600
So we have a couple
of splitters.

00:47:03.600 --> 00:47:05.310
In this case, I have
the Split Leader

00:47:05.310 --> 00:47:08.100
in zone A and split
zero, which gives me

00:47:08.100 --> 00:47:12.150
my data for the
row of the key 100.

00:47:12.150 --> 00:47:14.280
And it acquires a read lock.

00:47:14.280 --> 00:47:17.550
Then we do the lookups
for the other keys

00:47:17.550 --> 00:47:19.020
where we want to write to.

00:47:19.020 --> 00:47:22.800
We send the commit request
to the Split Leaders.

00:47:22.800 --> 00:47:25.500
And while we are doing this,
we choose one coordinator

00:47:25.500 --> 00:47:27.510
for this transaction,
who is basically

00:47:27.510 --> 00:47:30.990
the chef for this transaction.

00:47:30.990 --> 00:47:35.280
And we tag all
these Split Leaders

00:47:35.280 --> 00:47:37.252
as part of this transaction.

00:47:37.252 --> 00:47:38.710
So now, the first
thing that we do,

00:47:38.710 --> 00:47:40.650
because we want to
write to key 100, is we

00:47:40.650 --> 00:47:42.067
actually have to
check if we still

00:47:42.067 --> 00:47:44.100
have the read lock
on 100, and we have

00:47:44.100 --> 00:47:46.920
to elevate it to a write lock.

00:47:46.920 --> 00:47:49.590
Now, if there's any
conflicts, any possibility

00:47:49.590 --> 00:47:52.980
of a deadlock, what we
do is a wait and wound.

00:47:52.980 --> 00:47:54.840
So basically,
younger transactions

00:47:54.840 --> 00:47:56.220
are boarded and restarted.

00:47:56.220 --> 00:47:59.100
Older transactions are the
ones that are succeeding.

00:47:59.100 --> 00:48:02.280
So once we have all
the request lock,

00:48:02.280 --> 00:48:04.320
this is basically
the first phase

00:48:04.320 --> 00:48:07.050
of the two-phase
commit protocol.

00:48:07.050 --> 00:48:11.550
We can push the data out
to our split replicas

00:48:11.550 --> 00:48:14.130
and acknowledge, of course,
that we have the locks.

00:48:14.130 --> 00:48:15.570
Assign a timestamp.

00:48:15.570 --> 00:48:20.340
Again, replicate to the
other replicas of the splits.

00:48:20.340 --> 00:48:21.720
And then everybody
is coming back

00:48:21.720 --> 00:48:24.100
with the commit for
this transaction.

00:48:24.100 --> 00:48:28.440
And once we have all the commit
transactions notifications,

00:48:28.440 --> 00:48:30.690
we wait out the
TrueTime timestamp

00:48:30.690 --> 00:48:36.000
and then give the success
transaction back to the client.

00:48:36.000 --> 00:48:37.770
I have one minute left.

00:48:37.770 --> 00:48:43.500
I don't know if I get cut off
in my audio when I run over.

00:48:43.500 --> 00:48:46.050
So I want to do a really
quick demo, because I think

00:48:46.050 --> 00:48:48.640
it is actually fairly powerful.

00:48:48.640 --> 00:48:52.170
So let's see if I
get that working.

00:48:52.170 --> 00:48:55.390
So I do need to do
a couple of things.

00:48:55.390 --> 00:48:59.460
Number one is here I
have Grafana running.

00:48:59.460 --> 00:49:01.890
And what I'm using
here, just to explain it

00:49:01.890 --> 00:49:06.930
in very simple terms, is
I use the YCSB workload.

00:49:06.930 --> 00:49:08.310
And let me see.

00:49:08.310 --> 00:49:11.500
I actually need to
fix something here.

00:49:11.500 --> 00:49:13.990
Generate some data.

00:49:13.990 --> 00:49:17.760
So what I have is,
in Kubernetes, I

00:49:17.760 --> 00:49:21.900
have some clients running,
which use the YCSB

00:49:21.900 --> 00:49:24.320
schema of user
table, and I'm going

00:49:24.320 --> 00:49:28.330
to write to these user tables.

00:49:28.330 --> 00:49:33.210
So let's do demo03, and
then I also do demo04.

00:49:33.210 --> 00:49:36.180
So what this is doing
here is nothing else

00:49:36.180 --> 00:49:38.880
than I'm sending a pop-up
message, actually, which

00:49:38.880 --> 00:49:40.190
my clients are listening to.

00:49:40.190 --> 00:49:43.290
And they can get instructions
through this pop-up message.

00:49:43.290 --> 00:49:45.340
And the pop-up
message here says,

00:49:45.340 --> 00:49:48.390
I want to do random reads with
a target of 50 per client,

00:49:48.390 --> 00:49:50.040
and I want to do
random stale reads

00:49:50.040 --> 00:49:52.530
with a target of 50 per client.

00:49:52.530 --> 00:49:57.030
And we're running that against
two different instances.

00:49:57.030 --> 00:50:00.180
I want to show you the
power of unbounded reads.

00:50:00.180 --> 00:50:02.910
So one thing that we also
see is if you sometimes

00:50:02.910 --> 00:50:05.390
see even a read-only
transaction,

00:50:05.390 --> 00:50:08.090
but a read-only
strong transaction,

00:50:08.090 --> 00:50:12.360
if it conflicts with your write,
it could sometimes end up slow.

00:50:12.360 --> 00:50:15.830
So if you have the possibility
to do time-bounded queries

00:50:15.830 --> 00:50:17.870
where you allow a
little bit of staleness,

00:50:17.870 --> 00:50:22.130
even if it's less than a second
or a second of staleness,

00:50:22.130 --> 00:50:24.530
if you can accommodate
that in your application,

00:50:24.530 --> 00:50:27.950
do this, because it will speed
up your queries tremendously

00:50:27.950 --> 00:50:30.800
and helps basically
with lock contention

00:50:30.800 --> 00:50:32.790
if you see things like that.

00:50:32.790 --> 00:50:35.360
So what do we have here?

00:50:35.360 --> 00:50:37.340
Let me update this.

00:50:37.340 --> 00:50:39.710
Let's see if Grafana
is still happy with me.

00:50:39.710 --> 00:50:41.880
It looks like it's still happy.

00:50:41.880 --> 00:50:44.730
So we see here, right
now it's starting to run.

00:50:44.730 --> 00:50:47.580
So this was on a cold instance.

00:50:47.580 --> 00:50:50.950
It hasn't done much
for hours, basically.

00:50:50.950 --> 00:50:52.670
And I just started
this workload.

00:50:52.670 --> 00:50:54.380
And then what you
can see here is,

00:50:54.380 --> 00:50:56.900
basically, I have
clients in Asia,

00:50:56.900 --> 00:50:59.540
in Europe, and in
the United States

00:50:59.540 --> 00:51:05.910
which are sending write
requests against the database.

00:51:05.910 --> 00:51:08.600
So you can see here, in the
US, it's 33 milliseconds.

00:51:08.600 --> 00:51:11.180
In Asia, it's 210
round trip time.

00:51:11.180 --> 00:51:12.920
And then Europe is 88.

00:51:12.920 --> 00:51:15.110
So this is just
single cell writes.

00:51:15.110 --> 00:51:18.350
So this is basically
law of physics latency.

00:51:18.350 --> 00:51:20.110
These are strong reads.

00:51:20.110 --> 00:51:22.430
So I always have to
check against the leader

00:51:22.430 --> 00:51:25.650
of that split if I
have the latest data.

00:51:25.650 --> 00:51:29.070
Now, if I do time-bounded,
you see, there

00:51:29.070 --> 00:51:31.010
is not much difference here.

00:51:31.010 --> 00:51:35.030
The reason here is, this
is actually in the S--

00:51:35.030 --> 00:51:36.100
that instance.

00:51:36.100 --> 00:51:38.240
It doesn't have
replicas globally.

00:51:38.240 --> 00:51:40.570
It just have basically
multi-region US.

00:51:40.570 --> 00:51:41.945
So you still have
to go to the US

00:51:41.945 --> 00:51:45.320
to get your data if you
look at the latencies

00:51:45.320 --> 00:51:47.360
from Asia or from Europe.

00:51:47.360 --> 00:51:49.490
Now, let me show what
that means if you actually

00:51:49.490 --> 00:51:52.670
use a multi-regional
global database.

00:51:52.670 --> 00:51:55.700
Here in this case-- and I want
to zoom in to actually show you

00:51:55.700 --> 00:51:58.560
this a little bit nicer.

00:51:58.560 --> 00:51:59.870
So what do we have here?

00:51:59.870 --> 00:52:03.860
Here I have a global database
where I have read-only replicas

00:52:03.860 --> 00:52:05.910
in Asia and in Europe.

00:52:05.910 --> 00:52:09.110
So you see that the
times for reading strong

00:52:09.110 --> 00:52:10.460
is still about the same.

00:52:10.460 --> 00:52:12.420
It's a little lower,
as you can see.

00:52:12.420 --> 00:52:15.020
So sometimes you just don't
need to move any data.

00:52:15.020 --> 00:52:17.820
You need just to check if you
still have the latest data.

00:52:17.820 --> 00:52:19.700
And then you do the random read.

00:52:19.700 --> 00:52:21.020
So 10 milliseconds.

00:52:21.020 --> 00:52:22.400
From Asia, 166.

00:52:22.400 --> 00:52:24.450
From Europe, about 100.

00:52:24.450 --> 00:52:26.240
But then we go down here.

00:52:26.240 --> 00:52:28.910
And as you can see
here, I do time-bounded,

00:52:28.910 --> 00:52:31.170
and they are all about
nine milliseconds.

00:52:31.170 --> 00:52:33.710
So that is that
power of time-bounded

00:52:33.710 --> 00:52:36.800
reads with Cloud Spanner.

00:52:36.800 --> 00:52:41.540
Moving to the related
sessions, just two things

00:52:41.540 --> 00:52:44.030
that I want to point out.

00:52:44.030 --> 00:52:47.990
Today at 3:30 and then
tomorrow at 10:20,

00:52:47.990 --> 00:52:50.540
there are two more
sessions on Spanner.

00:52:50.540 --> 00:52:51.390
Really interesting.

00:52:51.390 --> 00:52:54.920
The first one is Andrew
Fikes, the VP of engineering.

00:52:54.920 --> 00:52:56.450
And then the second
one is showing

00:52:56.450 --> 00:53:00.620
how Google Cloud Storage has
actually built the metadata

00:53:00.620 --> 00:53:02.600
services built on
top of Cloud Spanner,

00:53:02.600 --> 00:53:06.240
and what qualities
gives that to you.

00:53:06.240 --> 00:53:10.990
And with that, there are
a couple of resources.

00:53:10.990 --> 00:53:13.080
The best one is
cloud.google.com/spanner.

00:53:13.080 --> 00:53:15.800
It gives you basically an
entry point into everything.

00:53:15.800 --> 00:53:18.170
If you want to learn a little
bit about optimizations,

00:53:18.170 --> 00:53:21.260
I have a video series under
"TIL about Cloud Spanner."

00:53:21.260 --> 00:53:25.490
So today, "Things I Learned
About Cloud Spanner," what

00:53:25.490 --> 00:53:26.900
you can see here basically.

00:53:26.900 --> 00:53:29.720
